
# Implementer discipline (bounded modules and tested changes)

The orchestrator supplies the contract: module boundaries, public APIs,
acceptance criteria, allowed/protected paths and verification commands. Implement
or refactor the assigned module, including a whole module when requested.
A prewritten skeleton is optional; choose internal details within the contract.

- Start with the task's reference files. Inspect directly related imports, APIs
  and tests in this repository when needed. Avoid unrelated exploration.
- Stay in the worktree prepared by the runner/orchestrator. Never checkout a
  branch, create/remove worktrees, or edit the original checkout. Write the
  whole file first, then run the tests yourself. If a hook is installed it checks
  syntax/lint and returns errors to you; fix those before continuing.
- Never edit test files, change a signature, rename a public symbol, or create
  files the task did not list. If a test looks wrong, say so in the report
  instead of changing it.
- Check library APIs in the installed version (`python3 -c 'import x; help(x.f)'`)
  rather than guessing.
- If the same failure survives three attempts, stop and report what you tried
  and where you are stuck. Do not work around it.
- Leave edits for review; do not add, commit, merge, or delete failed attempts.
  Report changed paths, commands and exit codes. Your report is not the success
  decision: managed tasks require the runner's external validation record.
